## Infrastructure explanation

### What is a server?
A server is a computer (physical or virtual) that provides data or services
to other computers called clients. A server runs an operating system (OS)
such as Linux. Servers are generally located in data centers, which are
secured and climate-controlled facilities with stable power supply and
high-speed network connections.

### What is the role of the domain name?
The domain name (foobar.com) is a human-readable alias that the DNS
translates into an IP address (8.8.8.8), so that machines can find each
other on the network.

### What type of DNS record is `www` in `www.foobar.com`?
`www.foobar.com` uses a DNS **A record**. An A record maps a domain name
directly to an IPv4 address. In this case, `www.foobar.com` resolves to
the IP address `8.8.8.8`.

### What is the role of the web server (Nginx)?
The web server (Nginx) receives incoming HTTP/HTTPS requests from the user's
browser and serves static content (HTML files, CSS, images). For dynamic
requests, it forwards them to the application server. The server communicates
with the user's computer over a network using the TCP/IP protocol.

### What is the role of the application server?
The application server executes the business logic and computes dynamic
content. It processes the requests forwarded by the web server, interacts
with the database if needed, and returns the generated HTML page back to
the web server.

### What is the role of the code base?
The code base contains all the application files (the source code). The
application server reads and executes these files to generate the dynamic
content.

### What is the role of the database (MySQL)?
The database stores and organizes the application data (users, posts,
orders, etc.). The application server sends SQL queries to retrieve or
update data, and the database returns the results.

### How does the server communicate with the user?
The server communicates with the user's computer over a network using the
**TCP/IP** protocol. The user's browser sends HTTP requests and the server
returns HTTP responses.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### SPOF (Single Point of Failure)
This infrastructure has a single server. If that server crashes or becomes
unavailable, the entire website goes down. Nothing is redundant: there is
no backup server, no failover mechanism.

### Downtime when maintenance is needed
When new code is deployed, the web server (Nginx) needs to be restarted.
During that restart, the website is temporarily unavailable. Any maintenance
operation on the single server causes downtime for the users.

### Cannot scale if too much incoming traffic
This infrastructure relies on a single server with fixed resources (CPU,
RAM). If the traffic exceeds the server's capacity (QPS ceiling), the server
will be overloaded and will not be able to handle all the requests. There is
no load balancer and no way to add servers dynamically to absorb traffic
spikes.